\begin_layout Section
Fallacy factors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The depth is a fallacy factor that should not be considered as complexity
 indicator, as the importance of a node not recognisable from its position.
 Besides arithmetic rules (e.g.
 commutativity, associativity) that could shift the node depth, it is not
 even clear whether nodes that are close to the root or close to the observation
s should be weighted more heavily.
 The maximum tree depth, which is often chosen as construction limit for
 trees, is also rather a shape specification, as the branching factor is
 not taken into account.
 This is also why the arity of operators should not be weighted additionally,
 their higher branching factor automatically increases the complexity in
 successing nodes.
\end_layout

